10. Termination and Cure

10.1 First inadvertent notice or citation omission

This subsection applies to a first breach consisting solely of an
inadvertently omitted license, attribution or patent notice, change
identification, or citation or acknowledgement required by section 3 or 7.1.
It does not cover intentional removal, concealment, or falsification of
notices, unauthorized Commercial Use, failure to obtain express acceptance
for a Research Model transfer, or a separate breach of another requirement.
A repeated breach is governed by section 10.2.

For a breach qualifying under this subsection, permissions do not terminate
if You fully correct it before receiving notice or within 30 days after
receipt of the Licensor's first written notice identifying it. If You do not
correct it within that period, permissions terminate at the end of the
period. They are automatically reinstated prospectively when You fully
correct that omission, even after the 30-day period, provided You have no
other uncured breach. No separate written reinstatement is required in that
case. Rights and claims arising during any period of termination remain
unaffected as stated in section 10.3.

For an already published work that You cannot directly amend, full cure is
satisfied by requesting a publisher correction where that process is
available and publishing a clearly linked correction on an accessible author
page, repository, or comparable public location identifying the work and
missing citation or acknowledgement. To avoid termination, do so within the
period above. A publisher's refusal or delay does not prevent cure if You
have taken these steps; publisher approval or implementation is not
required.

10.2 Other breaches and reinstatement

Other breaches terminate permissions immediately. Activities requiring those
permissions must stop. Commercial Use is not authorized by this license and
must not begin or continue without separate authorization. No cure period
grants temporary commercial permission.

For a first breach governed by this subsection, permissions are
automatically reinstated if You cease and fully cure that breach before
receiving notice or within 30 days after receipt of the Licensor's first
written notice identifying it, provided You have no other uncured breach.
After that period, or for a repeated breach, reinstatement requires the
Licensor's written agreement.

A repeated breach means a new violation of the same requirement after You
previously cured or obtained reinstatement for a violation of that
requirement. Section 10.1's automatic reinstatement after late correction
does not apply to a repeated breach or any other breach governed by this
subsection.

10.3 Effect on Research Models and accrued rights

Termination stops the permissions granted under sections 2.1 and 4,
including the patent permission for running Research Models. You may not
rely on those permissions while they are terminated. Obligations that
survive under section 7.3 are not a surviving copyright or patent license.

Termination does not transfer ownership of Your independently created model
or revoke rights You independently own, hold under another valid permission,
or have under applicable law. If an activity does not require a terminated
permission, termination alone does not prohibit that activity; the agreed
Research Model use, citation, and onward-transfer restrictions that survive
under section 7.3 still apply. Whether running a particular model requires
patent permission depends on the actual claims, relevant jurisdiction,
patent status, and applicable exceptions, not merely on its relationship to
RSSNet.

When permissions are reinstated under section 10.1 or 10.2, the grants in
sections 2.1 and 4 are restored prospectively within their original scope.
This includes the academic patent permission for Research Models to the
extent originally granted. Reinstatement creates no additional rights and
does not retroactively authorize activities during termination.

Cure or reinstatement does not authorize past Commercial Use or extinguish
claims arising before cure. Compliant recipients retain their direct
permissions even if an upstream distributor breaches this license. A
separate written agreement is governed by its own terms.

